entry fuel-rods
term: Fuel Rods
image: ../../assets/images/terms/fuel rods.webp
related_terms: nuclear-reactor
related_terms: uranium-235
related_terms: reactor-core
related_terms: defense-in-depth
quick_facts: Ceramic uranium-dioxide pellets inside zircon-alloy tubes
quick_facts: Pellets melt only around 2,800 °C and retain fission products
quick_facts: Rods bundle into assemblies standing in the reactor core
quick_facts: Cladding = second barrier of defense-in-depth
quick_facts: Replaced roughly every 4–6 years as spent fuel

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Nuclear Fuel Cycle","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle.aspx"}

definition:
    Long sealed metal tubes — typically zirconium alloy — packed with ceramic pellets of enriched uranium dioxide, each about the size of a fingertip. The rods are bundled into fuel assemblies that stand inside the reactor core, where fission in the pellets generates heat. The ceramic pellet survives temperatures near 2,800 °C and traps most fission products inside it; the cladding tube around it is the second physical barrier in defense-in-depth, keeping radioactive material sealed away from the coolant.

debunking_snippet:
    “Nuclear fuel rods are glowing green bars.” Real fuel looks like plain gray ceramic pellets stacked in metal tubes — it isn't even noticeably radioactive before it goes into the reactor.
